1. Deadlock
Definition

Deadlock is a situation where two or more processes are permanently waiting for resources held by each other, so none of them can continue.

Example

Suppose:

Process P1 → has Resource A → needs Resource B
Process P2 → has Resource B → needs Resource A
P1                         P2
 │                          │
 │ holds A                  │ holds B
 │                          │
 ↓                          ↓
needs B                   needs A
 │                          │
 └──────── waits ───────────┘

Neither can continue.

Four conditions for deadlock

All four generally need to be present:

Mutual Exclusion — resource can be used by only one process at a time.
Hold and Wait — process holds one resource while waiting for another.
No Preemption — resource cannot be forcibly taken away.
Circular Wait — processes wait for each other in a cycle.
How to handle deadlock

Common approaches:

Prevention — make one of the four conditions impossible.
Avoidance — allocate resources carefully, e.g. Banker's algorithm.
Detection and Recovery — detect deadlock and recover.
Ignore — some systems tolerate rare cases rather than continuously handling them.
