CPU Scheduling
Definition

CPU scheduling is the process of selecting which ready process/thread should get the CPU next.
Common scheduling algorithms
FCFS — First Come First Serve

The process that arrives first runs first.

P1 → P2 → P3

Simple, but a long process can make others wait.

SJF — Shortest Job First

The process requiring the shortest CPU burst runs first.

P2 = 2 ms
P1 = 5 ms
P3 = 8 ms

Order:
P2 → P1 → P3

Can provide low average waiting time, but long processes may starve.

Round Robin

Each process gets a fixed time quantum.

Example:

Quantum = 2 ms

P1 → 2 ms
P2 → 2 ms
P3 → 2 ms
P1 → 2 ms
...

Commonly associated with time-sharing systems.

Priority Scheduling

The process with the highest priority gets the CPU.

P1 → Priority 3
P2 → Priority 1
P3 → Priority 2

Depending on the convention, lower number may mean higher priority.

